%I A099730 #68 Nov 10 2026 22:35:37
%S A099730 1,5,3,21,13,7,85,53,29,9,341,213,117,37,11,1365,853,469,149,45,15,
%T A099730 5461,3413,1877,597,181,61,17,21845,13653,7509,2389,725,245,69,19,
%U A099730 87381,54613,30037,9557,2901,981,277,77,23,349525,218453,120149,38229,11605,3925,1109,309,93,25
%N A099730 Square array with T(n,0) = least odd number not in earlier rows, T(n,k+1) = 4*T(n,k)+1 (all having the same odd successor under 3x+1 iterations); n, k >= 0; read by falling antidiagonals.
%C A099730 Original name: Array read by antidiagonals. Rows contain odd numbers reaching same odd successor in Collatz function iteration.
%C A099730 All numbers that end in 3 will begin with numbers from previous row (for example, 3413 is 341 & 3). - _Jean-Bernard François_, Sep 09 2013 [Slightly incorrect, see below.]
%C A099730 The sequence is a permutation of the odd positive integers. - _Bob Selcoe_, Jul 26 2015
%C A099730 Every third term in each row is a multiple of 3 (because 4m+1 == m+1 (mod 3)), starting with the first, second or third depending on whether T(n,0) == 0, 2 or 1 (mod 3). Such terms have no odd ancestor for the reduced Collatz map (A075677), while the other terms have the odd ancestor A(x) = ((x-1)/3*4 + 1 if x == 1 (mod 3) else (x+1)/3*2 - 1), which is always the first term in a different row of the table. - _M. F. Hasler_, Oct 14 2026
%C A099730 The 2013 comment by JB François is slightly incorrect, for example, row 8 starts (23, 93, 373, ...). Here, 23 ends in 3 but no earlier row has a '2'; the remaining terms are those of row 3 (9, 37, 149, ...) with 3 appended; this is an earlier, not the directly previous row. Similarly for row 16 (43, 173, ...) etc. All terms of a given row indeed have either always the last digit 3, or the last digit alternating between 1 and 5, or between 7 and 9. Starting with 10m + 3 for any m will indeed give a trajectory under iterations of 4x+1 which equals that of m, with 3 appended to all values, because 4*3 + 1 = 10*1 + 3. - _M. F. Hasler_, Oct 27 2026
%H A099730 M. F. Hasler, <a href="/A099730/b099730.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..10010</a> (T(n,k) with n+k <= 140), Nov 04 2026
%F A099730 Let g(n) = floor((n+1)/3), then T(n,k) = 2^(2*k+1) * (n + g(n)) + (4^(k+1)-1)/3. - _Maon Wenders_, Jul 15 2012
%F A099730 T(n, k) = 4*T(n, k-1) + 1. - _Jean-Bernard François_, Sep 09 2013
%e A099730 T(1, 2) = 53 = 4*13 + 1; T(2, 5) = 7509 = 4*1877 + 1.
%e A099730 Array begins:
%e A099730    1   5   21    85   341   1365   5461   21845    87381 ...
%e A099730    3  13   53   213   853   3413  13653   54613   218453 ...
%e A099730    7  29  117   469  1877   7509  30037  120149   480597 ...
%e A099730    9  37  149   597  2389   9557  38229  152917   611669 ...
%e A099730   11  45  181   725  2901  11605  46421  185685   742741 ...
%e A099730   15  61  245   981  3925  15701  62805  251221  1004885 ...
%e A099730   17  69  277  1109  4437  17749  70997  283989  1135957 ...
%e A099730   19  77  309  1237  4949  19797  79189  316757  1267029 ...
%e A099730   ...
%e A099730 Construct the array by writing the odd numbers in the first column, skipping the third and then every fourth value, then fill the next columns in the same manner with odd numbers not used earlier. In the first column omit 5, 13, 21, 29, ... (always steps of 8 here); in the 2nd column omit 21, 53, 85, ... (always steps of 32), and so on. [Edited by _M. F. Hasler_, Oct 14 2026]
%t A099730 t[n_, k_] := 2^(2*(k + 1) - 1)*(n + Quotient[n + 1, 3]) + (4^(k + 1) - 1)/3; Table[t[n - k, k], {n, 0, 8}, {k, n, 0, -1}] // Flatten (* _Jean-François Alcover_, Sep 09 2013, after _Maon Wenders_ *)
%o A099730 (PARI) g(n)=(n+1)\3
%o A099730 T(n,k)=2^(2*(k+1)-1)*(n+g(n))+(4^(k+1)-1)/3
%o A099730 for(i=0,20,for(j=0,10,print1(T(i,j), ", ")); print()) \\ _Maon Wenders_, Jul 15 2012
%o A099730 (PARI) A099730(n,k)=(n\/3+n)<<(2*k+1)+4^(k+1)\3 \\ _M. F. Hasler_, Oct 13 2026
